## Documentation Changes

Documentation changes are important project changes.

This includes:

* Corrections to platform information
* Updated setup instructions
* New workflow documentation
* Changes to examples
* Changes to safety guidance
* Updated terminology
* Removed outdated instructions

If a documentation change materially affects how users should operate a workflow, record it here.

---

## Workflow Changes

When modifying an existing workflow, document the reason for the change.

For example:

```text
### Changed

- Updated the content queue workflow to require approval before scheduling.
- Added explicit failure handling for publishing errors.
- Added a weekly review step.
```

This makes it easier for contributors to understand why the workflow evolved.

---

## Free Version Information

Information about third-party software can change over time.

When documenting JarveePro free-version capabilities, contributors should avoid presenting old information as permanently accurate.

When appropriate, record significant documentation changes here and identify the date the information was reviewed.

The official JarveePro website should remain the reference point for current product availability, features, pricing, and platform support.

---

## Security Changes

Security-related changes should be clearly identified.

Examples:

```text
### Security

- Removed an accidentally exposed example credential.
- Updated dependency security guidance.
- Added instructions for handling authentication secrets.
```

Never place the actual secret or sensitive information in the changelog.

---

## Release Checklist

Before creating a significant release:

* [ ] Review documentation changes
* [ ] Review workflow changes
* [ ] Check for broken links
* [ ] Check examples for sensitive information
* [ ] Review security changes
* [ ] Update the version number
* [ ] Update this changelog
* [ ] Review the README if the project direction changed
* [ ] Test important workflows
* [ ] Review open issues and pull requests

---

## Project Principle

The changelog is more than a list of versions.

It is a record of what the community has learned.

When a workflow changes, document why.

When an assumption turns out to be wrong, document the correction.

When a better approach is discovered, document it.

That history helps future contributors avoid repeating the same mistakes.

